DHCP - you know what it is, but DORA is how it works
|D Discover
|O Offer
|R Request
|A Acknowledge
Client broadcasts a DHCP discover message seekign local DHCP server ->
-> Server responds with an offer message containing an IP for the client to accept ->
-> Client responds with a request message indicating acceptance of the offer ->
-> Server acknowledges response and registers that that IP is now assigned

ARP, or address resolution protocol, is used by nodes on the same LAN to determine eachother's MAC addresses for routing during a session. Node 1 wants to send traffic to Node2, so N1 will need to first determine what N2's MAC is. N1 will create a frame with her own MAC and send it to the broadcast MAC (ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff) along with the IP address of N2 whom she wants to speak to. Every device receives the broadcast. N2, recognizing it's MAC, will respond with her MAC address. From there, a handshake can occur and the session can begin.

